msgid "Qiskit Device Benchmarking, which also installs [Qiskit Experiments](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/) (`pip install git+https://github.com/qiskit-community/qiskit-device-benchmarking.git`)\n"
msgstr "Qiskit Device Benchmarking، التي تُثبّت أيضاً [Qiskit Experiments](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/) (`pip install git+https://github.com/qiskit-community/qiskit-device-benchmarking.git`)\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Setup {#setup}"
msgstr "الإعداد {#setup}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Import the required libraries.\n"
msgstr "استورد المكتبات المطلوبة.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The characterization suite builds several experiments, submits multiple jobs, and fits the results, which can take a couple of minutes in total. Enable INFO-level logging so that each stage prints its progress as it happens.\n"
msgstr "تبني حزمة التوصيف عدة تجارب، وتقدم مهاماً متعددة، وتلائم النتائج، مما قد يستغرق دقيقتين تقريباً في المجموع. فعّل التسجيل بمستوى INFO حتى تطبع كل مرحلة تقدمها أثناء حدوثه.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Small-scale simulator example {#small-scale-simulator-example}"
msgstr "مثال محاكي صغير النطاق {#small-scale-simulator-example}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This tutorial does not include a simulator example. The workflow measures the physical imperfections of a specific device: how quickly its qubits relax and dephase, and how often its gates and readout fail. An ideal simulator has none of these imperfections, so there is nothing to characterize. You could attach a synthetic noise model to a fake backend, but the experiments would then only recover the numbers you put in yourself. For that reason, we proceed directly to hardware, broken into the four steps of a Qiskit pattern.\n"
msgstr "لا يتضمن هذا الدرس مثالاً بمحاكي. يقيس سير العمل العيوب الفيزيائية لجهاز معين: مدى سرعة استرخاء كيوبتاته وفقدانها للطور، ومدى تكرار فشل بواباته وقراءته. المحاكي المثالي لا يحتوي على أي من هذه العيوب، فلا يوجد شيء لتوصيفه. يمكنك إرفاق نموذج ضوضاء اصطناعي بخلفية وهمية، لكن التجارب حينها ستستعيد فقط الأرقام التي أدخلتها بنفسك. لهذا السبب، ننتقل مباشرة إلى العتاد، مقسّماً إلى الخطوات الأربع لنمط Qiskit.\n"

#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Large-scale hardware example {#large-scale-hardware-example}"
msgstr "مثال عتاد على نطاق واسع {#large-scale-hardware-example}"

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"
msgstr "الخطوة 1: تحويل المدخلات الكلاسيكية إلى مسألة كمية {#step-1-map-classical-inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this workflow the \"problem\" is the device itself: the classical inputs are the QPU you want to characterize and the list of properties to measure, and the quantum experiments are the characterization circuits generated from them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "First, select a backend. This tutorial uses `ibm_kingston`, a 156-qubit Heron r2 processor; you can substitute any IBM Quantum device you have access to (or pick one with `service.least_busy()`).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Next, choose which properties to measure. You can pass any subset of the following experiments:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "`readout`: SPAM (state preparation and measurement) experiment\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "`rb_1q`: isolated single-qubit randomized benchmarking\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "`rb_2q`: simultaneous two-qubit randomized benchmarking from a layer fidelity experiment\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "`t2`: $T_2$ Hahn echo experiment (a single echo by default)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "`t1`: $T_1$ relaxation experiment\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Run the full suite to get a complete picture of the device; drop entries from the list if you only care about some properties and want to save QPU time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In most tutorials this is where you would transpile your circuits. Here, `BackendCharacterization` takes care of that internally. It builds the experiment circuits directly in the device's native gates, runs the single-qubit experiments on all qubits in parallel, and schedules the two-qubit benchmarking over disjoint layers of the coupling map so that the entire device is covered with a handful of jobs. Because of this parallelization, the whole suite consumes only about 70 seconds of QPU time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Initialize the class that manages the workflow:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-primitives}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `run_experiments` method builds the circuits and submits them as [Sampler](/guides/get-started-with-sampler) jobs. Run it inside a [`Batch`](/guides/run-jobs-batch) context so that the jobs are scheduled together on the QPU, and the method returns once all jobs have been submitted. (You can also run it outside a batch, or inside a `Session`.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"You might see a warning about a backend being passed while a session context manager is open. This is expected and can be safely ignored.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The method returns the submitted jobs as a dictionary keyed by experiment, which is useful for tracking them on the [IBM Quantum Platform dashboard](/guides/monitor-job) or for debugging.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format {#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Analyze the results {#analyze-the-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `analyze_results` method waits for the jobs to finish and fits the measurement data: exponential decays for $T_1$ and $T_2$, survival-probability decays for the RB experiments, and assignment matrices for readout. It returns a dictionary of *error maps*, one entry per measured property, each mapping a qubit (or qubit pair) to its measured value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The maps cover readout error, the single-qubit `x` and `sx` gate errors, the layered two-qubit errors (`lf_error_map`, from the layer fidelity experiment), and the $T_1$ and $T_2$ times in seconds.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Update the backend properties {#update-the-backend-properties}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `update_backend` method writes the measured values into the properties of a copy of the backend and returns it. The original `backend` object keeps the reported calibration data, so that we can compare the two, as shown below. Remember that this update is purely local to your Python session; it does not change anything on the device or for other users.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Compare the measured properties against the reported ones {#compare-the-measured-properties-against-the-reported-ones}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, plot the measured real-time properties against the values the backend reports. In each panel the qubits (or qubit pairs) are sorted by the measured value, so the measured curve is smooth by construction and the scatter of the reported values around it shows where the two disagree. A single plot is shown for single-qubit RB because the same measured error per gate is assigned to both the `x` and `sx` gates. Axis limits are chosen from the bulk of the data so that a few extreme outliers do not compress the plots; any points outside the range are counted in an annotation on the plot (or pass `ylim` to override the limits).\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-0.avif)\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-1.avif)\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-2.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-2.avif)\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-3.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-3.avif)\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-4.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-25-4.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "These plots require a careful reading, because not every gap between the two curves means the device has drifted:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Readout and single-qubit errors**: The measured values track the reported ones for most qubits, while the reported value for a handful of qubits is far off the measured curve. Those localized disagreements are the drift this workflow is designed to catch.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**$T_1$**: The two data sets scatter around each other with no systematic offset, which is consistent with $T_1$ fluctuating naturally over time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**Two-qubit errors**: The measured values sit systematically *above* the reported ones. This is expected because the reported values are measured on isolated gates, while the layer fidelity experiment runs many gates simultaneously and therefore includes crosstalk. The layered numbers are more relevant for deep circuits, but they are not directly comparable to the reported calibration data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "**$T_2$**: The measured values sit well *below* the reported ones for most qubits. Again, methodology plays a large role, as examined in the next section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inspect individual experiment results {#inspect-individual-experiment-results}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "You can also inspect the raw results of the individual characterization experiments through the `experiment_data` property, which returns the underlying [Qiskit Experiments](https://qiskit-community.github.io/qiskit-experiments/) `ExperimentData` objects. For example, the following displays the measured $T_1$ decay curve of a single qubit, which is useful for checking the quality of a fit before trusting the number it produced.\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-28-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-28-1.avif)\n"

#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measure T2 with a dynamical decoupling train {#measure-t2-with-a-dynamical-decoupling-train}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "By default the $T_2$ experiment applies a single Hahn echo and measures all qubits in parallel, which, as the comparison plot above showed, can yield noticeably lower $T_2$ values than the backend reports. One hypothesis is the number of echoes: the reported values may be calibrated with dynamical decoupling, which suppresses low-frequency noise. To test this, run a $T_2$-only characterization with `t2_num_echoes` set to a larger value, which applies a CPMG-style train of echo pulses. The delays represent the total free-evolution time in both cases, so the fitted $T_2$ values are directly comparable to the single-echo results.\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-31-0.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/refresh-backend-properties-with-real-time-benchmarking/extracted-outputs/cell-31-0.avif)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In this run the extra echoes barely changed the result: the 8-echo median (56 &micro;s) is close to the single-echo one (53 &micro;s), and both remain far below the reported median (142 &micro;s). The number of echoes alone does not explain this gap; other methodological differences are involved, such as calibration procedure details, measuring all qubits in parallel rather than in isolation, and so forth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The practical lesson is to treat $T_2$ (and layered two-qubit error) comparisons across methodologies with care. The refreshed values are a self-consistent snapshot taken under conditions close to a real workload, which makes them well suited for comparing qubits against each other or tracking a device over time. A gap relative to the reported calibration data, however, is not automatically evidence of drift.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Steps 1–4 compressed into a single call {#steps-14-compressed-into-a-single-call}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For day-to-day use you rarely need the intermediate results. The `run_and_update` method chains everything you did above (building, submitting, fitting, and updating) into a single call that returns the refreshed backend. It accepts the same `experiments` list (and forwards options such as `t2_num_echoes`), and can likewise be wrapped in a `Batch` or `Session` context.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"The following cell does not return until all jobs and post-processing are done.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The refreshed `backend_updated` object is now ready to be used wherever backend properties feed into your workflow. Try out this process in conjunction with the [Real-time benchmarking for qubit selection](/tutorials/real-time-benchmarking-for-qubit-selection) tutorial to choose the best-performing qubits when transpiling a circuit. Because the device keeps drifting, refresh the properties close to the time you actually execute your workload.\n"
msgstr ""

